ek, Blende, Pueblo\nReservoir, Pueblo West, Avondale, Pinon, Pueblo Depot, Greenhorn\nMountain, Silver Cliff, Rye, Colorado City, Beulah, San Isabel\nand Rosita.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-08-25T20:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Wet Mountains between 6300 and 10000Ft; Wet Mountains above 10000 Ft; Pueblo Vicinity/Pueblo County Below 6300 Feet","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.430b234ba5523821e50ea9a05b02d959fb79831f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.430b234ba5523821e50ea9a05b02d959fb79831f.001.1"},{"event":"Flash Flood Watch","headline":"Flash Flood Watch issued August 25 at 5:15PM MDT until August 26 at 9:00PM MDT by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flash flooding and debris flows caused by excessive rainfall\ncontinue to be possible over the Aspen Acres burn scar.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of southeast Colorado, including the following\nareas, Pueblo Vicinity/Pueblo County Below 6300 Feet, Wet Mountains\nAbove 10000 Feet and Wet Mountains between 6300 and 10000 Feet.\n\n* WHEN...Until 8 PM MDT this evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Heavy rainfall over the Aspen Acres burn scar is expected\nup to and during the period of the watch. Residents near the Aspen\nAcres burn scar should prepare for potential debris flow. Be sure\nto stay up to date with information from local authorities. Heavy\nrainfall could trigger flash flooding of low-lying areas, urbanized\nstreet flooding, and debris flows in and near recent wildfire burn\nscars.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- National Weather Service Meteorologists are forecasting heavy\nrainfall over the burn scar, which may lead to flash flooding and\ndebris flows.\n- Some locations that may experience flash flooding include...\nPueblo, Pueblo Airport, Boone, Salt Creek, Blende, Pueblo\nReservoir, Pueblo West, Avondale, Pinon, Pueblo Depot, Greenhorn\nMountain, Silver Cliff, Rye, Colorado City, Beulah, San Isabel\nand Rosita.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","e